\tag{fcap}
\tag{clifford}
\tag{draft}

\taxon{Convention}
\title{Two coordinate orders for the degenerate projective plane}

\p{Work over #{\mathbb R} with an orthogonal frame #{e_0,e_1,e_2} and

##{Q(ae_0+be_1+ce_2)=b^2+c^2.}

Thus #{e_0^2=0}, #{e_1^2=e_2^2=1}, and distinct generators anticommute.
Suppress the canonical generator map in products and write
#{e_{ij}=e_ie_j} and #{I=e_{012}}. This is the full degenerate Clifford
algebra: no radical quotient is taken, #{I^2=0}, and #{I} will never be
inverted. The Euclidean row of Sokolov's metric table fixes this convention
\citet{Section 2.4, p. 20}{sokolov2013foundations}.}

\p{We will compare two mathematical basis orders. Sokolov's displayed order
is

##{S=(1,e_0,e_1,e_2,e_{12},e_{20},e_{01},e_{012}),}

whereas the ascending-mask order used by the preceding coordinate cards is

##{C=(1,e_0,e_1,e_{01},e_2,e_{02},e_{12},e_{012}).}

The only change of blade orientation is
#{e_{20}=-e_{02}}. These are declared mathematical orders; neither is an
assertion about any software library's physical storage or iteration order.
Sokolov's basis table appears with his duality calculation in
\citet{Section 2.6, p. 25}{sokolov2013foundations}.}

\p{Use nonzero homogeneous representatives

##{L(d,a,b)=de_0+ae_1+be_2,\qquad
P(w,x,y)=we_{12}+xe_{20}+ye_{01}}

for a projective line and point. A finite point has #{w\ne0} and may be
normalized to #{w=1}; #{e_0} is the ideal line, and every nonzero
#{P(0,x,y)} is an ideal point. These encodings and their ideal elements are
the source conventions in
\citet{Section 2.9, pp. 32--33}{sokolov2013foundations}.}
